{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_15","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/zsh -lc 'cat CLAUDE.md README.md'","aggregated_output":"# Homelab - Claude Code Context\n\n## Project overview\n\nTerraform-managed homelab on a 3-node Proxmox cluster (pve01, pve02, pve03).\nThe primary active project is a K3s cluster on pve03, being evolved toward managed\nKubernetes parity. See `docs/` for phased plans.\n\n## Proxmox API access (read-only)\n\nEndpoint: `https://192.168.1.138:8006` (pve03 - any node works)\nToken: `claude@pam!claudeReadOnly=871b29f8-0975-413f-9261-fb8d0bd54798`\nSelf-signed cert - always use `-k` with curl.\n\n```bash\n# Example: node status\ncurl -sk -H \"Authorization: PVEAPIToken=claude@pam!claudeReadOnly=871b29f8-0975-413f-9261-fb8d0bd54798\" \\\n  https://192.168.1.138:8006/api2/json/nodes/pve03/status | python3 -m json.tool\n\n# Example: storage pools\ncurl -sk -H \"Authorization: PVEAPIToken=claude@pam!claudeReadOnly=871b29f8-0975-413f-9261-fb8d0bd54798\" \\\n  https://192.168.1.138:8006/api2/json/nodes/pve03/storage | python3 -m json.tool\n\n# Example: VM list\ncurl -sk -H \"Authorization: PVEAPIToken=claude@pam!claudeReadOnly=871b29f8-0975-413f-9261-fb8d0bd54798\" \\\n  https://192.168.1.138:8006/api2/json/nodes/pve03/qemu | python3 -m json.tool\n\n# Example: LXC list\ncurl -sk -H \"Authorization: PVEAPIToken=claude@pam!claudeReadOnly=871b29f8-0975-413f-9261-fb8d0bd54798\" \\\n  https://192.168.1.138:8006/api2/json/nodes/pve03/lxc | python3 -m json.tool\n```\n\n## Infrastructure facts (verified via API)\n\n### pve03 (Dell PowerEdge R620)\n- CPU: 2 x Intel Xeon E5-2630L v2 @ 2.40 GHz (24 cores total)\n- RAM: 62.8 GB total\n- OS disk: /dev/sda - Seagate ST9250315AS 250 GB HDD (BIOS boot, `local` + `local-lvm`)\n- `local-lvm`: 147 GB total, thin LVM on the HDD, currently 135 GB free (slow - not for K3s storage)\n- `data02`: 482 GB ZFS RAIDZ pool across 3 x Samsung 860 EVO 250 GB SSDs (/dev/sdb/c/d, all 99% health)\n  - Currently: 218 GB used, 265 GB free\n  - After planned cleanup (runners + debiantmpl): ~420 GB free\n\n### K3s cluster (all VMs on pve03/data02)\n| VM | ID | Role | vmbr0 IP | vmbr1 IP | RAM (actual) |\n|----|-----|------|----------|----------|-------------|\n| k3sserver01 | 411 | server + control-plane (NoSchedule) | 192.168.1.201 | 172.16.1.1 | 5.6 GB / 9.4 GB max |\n| k3sagent01 | 421 | agent | 192.168.1.211 | 172.16.2.1 | 7.1 GB / 9.4 GB max |\n| k3sagent02 | 422 | agent | 192.168.1.212 | 172.16.2.2 | 7.7 GB / 9.4 GB max |\n| k3snodetmpl | 113 | template (full clone of debiantmpl) | - | - | stopped |\n| debiantmpl | 112 | template (no ZFS dependents - safe to delete) | - | - | stopped |\n\n- k3sserver01/agent01/agent02 are **linked clones** of k3snodetmpl\n- k3snodetmpl is a **full clone** of debiantmpl (no ZFS dependency chain)\n- K3s cluster domain: `homelab.cluster` (not `cluster.local`)\n- CNI: Cilium 1.17.1, native routing over ens19 (vmbr1)\n\n### GitHub runners (pve03, LXC containers 103/104/106-111)\n- 8 x unprivileged Debian LXC containers on data02, running Docker for GitHub Actions\n- Actual disk usage: ~120 GB total (~15 GB each)\n- RAM usage: ~170 MB each (basically idle)\n- **Planned for removal** - to be replaced by Actions Runner Controller in K3s\n\n### Proxmox nodes\nCluster name: **KingsRoad**\n\n| Node | Role | IP (vmbr0) |\n|------|------|------------|\n| pve01 | Proxmox node | 192.168.1.130 |\n| pve02 | Proxmox node | 192.168.1.131 |\n| pve03 | Proxmox node (K3s host) | 192.168.1.138 |\n\n### Other hosts\n- pve02: truenas01 (VM 102, ZFS NAS - ~50 GB free, not suitable for K3s storage currently), ubuntu01 (VM 105, 476 GB SanDisk SSD passthrough)\n- pve01: ubuntu02 (VM 101, Intel GVTg GPU passthrough for transcoding)\n\n## Repository layout\n\n```\nproxmox/                  Terraform - Proxmox VMs, LXCs, firewall, DNS\n  modules/\n    k3s-node/             Reusable module for K3s VM (linked clone of template)\n    gh-runner/            LXC container module for GitHub runners\nservices/k3s/\n  kubernetes/             Terraform - K3s workloads (Helm releases, manifests)\n    modules/              One module per concern\n  ansible/                K3s node provisioning\ndocs/\n  001-upgrade-to-managed-lite/   Phased plan: Cilium, observability, RBAC, Vault, registry\n  003-persistent-storage/        Active plan: Longhorn persistent storage\n```\n\n## Active work\n\nSee `docs/003-persistent-storage/PROGRESS.md` - deploying Longhorn for persistent\nstorage, including cleanup of runner LXCs, RAM bumps, adding k3sagent03, and\nmigrating existing PVCs off local-path.\n\n## Key conventions\n\n- One Terraform module per concern in `services/k3s/kubernetes/modules/`\n- Helm values use heredoc: `values = [<<-YAML ... YAML]` with `${var.foo}` interpolation\n- All Helm releases: `wait = true`, `timeout = 300` (600 for heavy charts)\n- Sensitive variables: declared in `variables.tf`, passed via `terraform.tfvars` (gitignored)\n- `kubernetes_manifest` resources that conflict with existing field managers: add `field_manager { force_conflicts = true }`\n- Firewall is staged but disabled on all K3s VMs - rules exist, `enabled = false`\n- Cluster domain is `homelab.cluster` - hardcode this in any service DNS URLs\n# Homelab\n\nTerraform-managed homelab on a 3-node Proxmox cluster (pve01/pve02/pve03).\nThe primary active workload is a K3s cluster on pve03, running on three agent\nnodes with Longhorn distributed block storage.\n\n## Repository layout\n\n```\nterraform/\n  s3-backend/                     Provisions S3 bucket + DynamoDB lock table for remote state\n\nproxmox/                          Terraform - Proxmox VMs, LXCs, firewall, DNS\n  modules/\n    k3s-node/                     Reusable VM module (linked clone of template)\n    gh-runner/                    LXC module for GitHub Actions runners\n\nproxmox-databases/                Terraform - Shared database LXC containers (pve03/data02)\n  modules/\n    db-server/                    Reusable LXC + install-script module\n\nnetwork/                          Terraform - UniFi DHCP reservations (driven by remote state)\n\nservices/k3s/\n  kubernetes/                     Terraform - K3s cluster infrastructure\n    modules/\n      arc-controller/             GitHub Actions Runner Controller (ARC)\n      arc-runners/                ARC RunnerScaleSet\n      cert-manager/               PKI chain (self-signed root -> intermediary CA)\n      certificate/                TLS cert (cert-manager) + Traefik IngressRoute\n      cilium/                     CNI: Cilium in native routing mode\n      external-secrets/           External Secrets Operator + Vault ClusterSecretStore\n      gitea/                      Self-hosted Git forge + container registry + Actions CI\n      headlamp/                   Kubernetes dashboard UI\n      longhorn/                   Distributed block storage (default StorageClass)\n      loki/                       Log aggregation (Loki + Promtail)\n      metallb/                    Bare-metal LoadBalancer\n      metrics-server/             Resource metrics for kubectl top / HPA\n      namespace/                  Per-app isolation bundle (Namespace + RBAC + quotas)\n      prometheus/                 Metrics + alerting + Grafana dashboards\n      public-certificate/         TLS cert for public-facing (Let's Encrypt) ingresses\n      traefik-dashboard/          Traefik ingress dashboard (internal)\n      traefik-public/             Traefik instance for public-facing ingresses\n      vault/                      HashiCorp Vault (secrets backend for ESO)\n      wildcard-cert/              Wildcard cert for *.lab.richardbenson.co.uk\n    apps/                         Per-app Terraform - one file per app (see below)\n  ansible/                        K3s node provisioning playbooks\n\ndocs/\n  001-upgrade-to-managed-lite/    Phased plan: Cilium, observability, RBAC, Vault, registry\n  003-persistent-storage/         Completed plan: Longhorn storage migration\n  008-ci-runners/                 Phased plan: ARC + Gitea act_runner\n  009-dependency-upgrades/        Phased plan: Helm chart + provider version bumps\n```\n\n## Workspaces and remote state\n\nAll workspaces use S3 remote state (`rbenson-homelab-tfstate`, eu-west-2) with\nDynamoDB locking. Workspaces read from each other via `terraform_remote_state`:\n\n| Workspace | S3 key | Reads from |\n|-----------|--------|------------|\n| `terraform/s3-backend/` | local only | - |\n| `proxmox/` | `proxmox/terraform.tfstate` | - |\n| `proxmox-databases/` | `proxmox-databases/terraform.tfstate` | - |\n| `services/k3s/kubernetes/` | `k3s-kubernetes/terraform.tfstate` | proxmox-databases |\n| `network/` | `network/terraform.tfstate` | proxmox, proxmox-databases |\n\n## Cluster at a glance\n\n| Node | Role | LAN IP | Internal IP |\n|------|------|--------|-------------|\n| k3sserver01 | control-plane (NoSchedule) | 192.168.1.201 | 172.16.1.1 |\n| k3sagent01 | agent | 192.168.1.211 | 172.16.2.1 |\n| k3sagent02 | agent | 192.168.1.212 | 172.16.2.2 |\n| k3sagent03 | agent | 192.168.1.213 | 172.16.2.3 |\n\nCNI: Cilium 1.19.2, native routing over `ens19`. Cluster domain: `homelab.cluster`.\n\n## Database containers (pve03/data02)\n\nStandalone LXC containers simulating a managed cloud database topology - apps in\nK3s connect over TCP rather than running in-cluster DB sidecars.\n\n| CT | Hostname | IP | Software |\n|----|----------|----|----------|\n| 120 | postgres01 | 192.168.1.220 | PostgreSQL (Gitea + future apps) |\n| 121 | valkey01 | 192.168.1.221 | Valkey 8 (sessions/cache) |\n| 122 | mariadb01 | 192.168.1.222 | MariaDB 10.11 |\n| 123 | mongodb01 | 192.168.1.223 | MongoDB 7.0 |\n\nApp databases and users are provisioned by the relevant Terraform module (not by\nthe `proxmox-databases/` workspace itself). DHCP reservations are managed\nautomatically by `network/` via remote state - no manual UniFi changes needed\nwhen adding a new container.\n\n## Services\n\n| Service | URL |\n|---------|-----|\n| Gitea | https://gitea.lab.richardbenson.co.uk |\n| Grafana | https://grafana.lab.richardbenson.co.uk |\n| Prometheus | https://prometheus.lab.richardbenson.co.uk |\n| Alertmanager | https://alertmanager.lab.richardbenson.co.uk |\n| Longhorn UI | https://longhorn.lab.richardbenson.co.uk |\n| Headlamp | https://headlamp.lab.richardbenson.co.uk |\n| Vault | https://vault.lab.richardbenson.co.uk |\n| Traefik dashboard | https://traefik.lab.richardbenson.co.uk |\n\n## Applying infrastructure changes\n\nEach workspace is applied independently. Always run `terraform plan` first.\n\n```bash\n# K3s cluster workloads (most common)\ncd services/k3s/kubernetes\nterraform plan\nterraform apply\n\n# Proxmox VMs / LXCs\ncd proxmox\nterraform plan\nterraform apply\n\n# Database containers\ncd proxmox-databases\nterraform plan\nterraform apply\n\n# UniFi DHCP reservations (run after proxmox or proxmox-databases changes)\ncd network\nterraform plan\nterraform apply\n```\n\nSensitive variables are in `terraform.tfvars` (gitignored) in each workspace.\nSee `variables.tf` for required keys. See `terraform.tfvars.example` for the\nfull list of expected values.\n\n---\n\n## How to add a new app\n\nEach app is a single self-contained file in `services/k3s/kubernetes/apps/`.\nAdding an app = create the file, plan, apply. Removing an app = delete the file,\nplan, apply.\n\n### 1. Create the app file\n\nCreate `services/k3s/kubernetes/apps/<appname>.tf`:\n\n```hcl\nmodule \"namespace_myapp\" {\n  source   = \"../modules/namespace\"\n  app_name = \"myapp\"\n}\n```\n\nThis provisions the full isolation bundle in one step:\n- **Namespace** `myapp`\n- **ResourceQuota** - default: 500m/2 CPU, 256Mi/1Gi memory across all pods\n- **LimitRange** - default per-container: 100m/500m CPU, 64Mi/256Mi memory\n- **ServiceAccount** `myapp-sa`\n- **Role + RoleBinding** - scoped read/write access for the ServiceAccount\n\n#### Adding ingress\n\nIf the app needs a public URL, add the `certificate` module:\n\n```hcl\nmodule \"namespace_myapp\" {\n  source   = \"../modules/namespace\"\n  app_name = \"myapp\"\n}\n\nmodule \"ingress_myapp\" {\n  source       = \"../modules/certificate\"\n  namespace    = \"myapp\"\n  hostname     = \"myapp.lab.richardbenson.co.uk\"\n  service_name = \"myapp\"       # Kubernetes Service name\n  service_port = 8080\n  depends_on   = [module.namespace_myapp]\n}\n```\n\nThen create a DNS record for the hostname pointing at the Traefik LoadBalancer IP:\n\n```bash\nkubectl get svc -n kube-system traefik -o jsonpath='{.status.loadBalancer.ingress[0].ip}'\n```\n\n#### Overriding quotas\n\n```hcl\nmodule \"namespace_myapp\" {\n  source         = \"../modules/namespace\"\n  app_name       = \"myapp\"\n  cpu_limit      = \"4\"\n  memory_limit   = \"4Gi\"\n}\n```\n\nAll variables: `services/k3s/kubernetes/modules/namespace/variables.tf`.\n\n### 2. Apply\n\n```bash\ncd services/k3s/kubernetes\nterraform plan\nterraform apply\n```\n\n### 3. Deploy the app\n\nPoint manifests or Helm chart at the namespace:\n\n```yaml\nmetadata:\n  namespace: myapp\n```\n\nUse the provisioned ServiceAccount:\n\n```yaml\nspec:\n  serviceAccountName: myapp-sa\n```\n\n### 4. Persistent storage\n\nOmit `storageClassName` to get the default (`longhorn`, 2 replicas). For\ncache/scratch where loss is acceptable, use `longhorn-single` (1 replica).\nNever use `local-path` - data will be lost if the pod reschedules to a\ndifferent node.\n\n```yaml\napiVersion: v1\nkind: PersistentVolumeClaim\nmetadata:\n  name: myapp-data\n  namespace: myapp\nspec:\n  accessModes: [ReadWriteOnce]\n  resources:\n    requests:\n      storage: 5Gi\n  # storageClassName: longhorn-single  # cache/scratch only\n```\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
